.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH EXP 3M
.SH NAME
exp, log, log10, pow, sqrt \- exponential, logarithm, power, square root
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.B double exp(double x);
.PP
.B double log(double x);
.PP
.B double log10(double x);
.PP
.B double pow(double x, double y);
.PP
.B double sqrt(double x);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Exp
returns the exponential function of
.I x.
.PP
.I Log
returns the natural logarithm of
.IR x ;
.I log10
returns the base 10 logarithm.
.PP
.I Pow
returns
.I  x\u\s8y\s10\d.
.PP
.I Sqrt
returns the square root of
.I x.
.SH SEE ALSO
hypot(3), sinh(3), floor(3), intro(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Exp
and
.I pow
return HUGE_VAL when the correct value would
overflow;
.I errno
is set to ERANGE.
.I Pow
returns 0 and sets
.I errno
to
EDOM when the second argument is negative and non-integral and
when
both arguments are 0.
.PP
.I Log
returns \-HUGE_VAL when
.I x
is zero or negative;
.I errno
is set to EDOM.
.B Note:
v7 returned 0 here.
\-HUGE_VAL is the most negative finite value, which is as close to the true
\-infinity as this machine can come; see BESM-6 NOTES.
.PP
.I Sqrt
returns 0 when
.I x
is negative;
.I errno
is set to EDOM.
A
.I sqrt
of exactly zero returns zero and sets nothing.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The range gates are the port
On this machine an exponent that rises past 2**63 raises an arithmetic-overflow
fault and
.B the program dies;
it does not produce an infinity, because the format has none.  An exponent that
falls below 2**\-63 quietly becomes machine zero.  The two ends are therefore not
symmetric, and HUGE_VAL is a value a routine
.I returns,
never one it computes: every range check in
.I libm
is made
.I before
the arithmetic that would overflow, and is checked strictly.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 tested
.I exp
against a single
.I maxf
of DBL_MAX_10_EXP*2.5 = 45, but e**45 is about 3.5e19 \- past this machine's
largest finite 9.223e18, so computing it faulted.
.I Exp
now has two gates instead, and each is placed before the arithmetic:
.IP \(bu 3n
overflow above
.B x = 43.6.
ln(DBL_MAX) is 43.668, and 43.6 is the last tenth that keeps the closing
.IR ldexp (3)
in range.  Past it: ERANGE and HUGE_VAL.
.IP \(bu 3n
underflow below
.B x = \-44.3.
ln(DBL_MIN) is \-44.36, and by \-44.3 the result has already flushed to machine
zero.  This gate is not cosmetic:
.IR ldexp (3)
keeps only the low seven bits of its argument, so an ungated tiny
.I x
would wrap the exponent and come back
.I enormous.
No
.I errno
is set \- an underflow to zero is what the hardware does to any small value
anyway.
.SS sqrt
v7 put the exponent back on by multiplying or dividing by 1L<<30 in a loop, which
is a way of writing
.IR ldexp (3)
on a machine whose library had none.  Here
.I ldexp
is six instructions and is exact.
The
.B float sqrtf(float)
that accompanied the v7 source is gone:
.BR float ,
.B double
and
.B "long double"
are the same one 48-bit word here, and <math.h> declares no `f'- or `l'-suffixed
variants at all.
.SS pow
A negative base has a real power only for an integer exponent, and v7 found out
by rounding the exponent to a
.B long
and comparing.  A
.B long
is one word here \- 41 bits signed \- while the exponent ranges to 2**63, so that
conversion is undefined for a large exponent and the test is gated by
.B fabs(y) < 2**40
first.  Above 2**40 every value is already integral, a 40-bit mantissa having no
fractional bits left.
.PP
.I pow(0, 0)
is EDOM, as in v7.  C11 sec 7.12.7.4 permits either 1 or a domain error.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libm.a
.br
lib/libm/exp.c, log.c, pow.c, sqrt.c
